> > didn't have any problems after I instructed: "/SEGMENTS:400".
>
> At least with Microsoft compilers, static data, constants and stack are
> grouped together (even in huge model, I think).  Using huge model will
> significantly affect performance;  you don't really need it, since there
> are no *single* data items bigger than 64k.  If you still have trouble,
> make all of the big static data items "far".  The switch -Gt60 for MSC
> will also help.

(1) I'm using MS C 5.1, with MS LINK 3.65.  Up until this little project I
was also using the MS linker, but, well... we won't mention what it said
about the makefile <GRIN>.  I downloaded NDMAKE instead of re-coding.

(2) the /SE:400 seems to help some... got more modules linked before it
blew up :-).

(3) -Gt60 might be one answer, but I just patched the makefile with
	-ND _DATA_$*  -AL -Au
as a CL option, which is A-bombs to -Gt60's firecrackers, right?  Obviously,
I don't usually use large model.  Small model does me just fine except...
So I didn't know (and I guess a lot of folks don't...) that when MS makes
Large model, they put all initialized data from all modules into one 
segment.  Sorta like dropping an H-bomb, but not arming it in case someone
might get hurt. 

